      <div class="head"><h3>Cup saucer: GoPro data on the iPhone</h3><p class="sub">Source: GoPro in the UMI mount · Target: iPhone · identical Default UMI fingers, no adaptation only</p></div>
      <div class="her-pair">
        <div class="ph"><div class="k">Source rollout · GoPro</div><div class="w">Third-person clip of the GoPro-equipped iPhUMI gripper placing the cup on the saucer.</div><div class="s">third person · 1920×1080 · 10–15 s · mp4</div></div>
        <div class="ph ok"><div class="k">Target rollout · iPhone</div><div class="w">Third-person clip of the iPhone-equipped gripper on the robot, finetuned on 25 iPhone demos.</div><div class="s">third person · 1920×1080 · 10–15 s · mp4</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="her-quad">
        <div class="tile"><div class="label"><i style="background:#5b6472"></i>No adaptation</div><div class="ph"><div class="k">Media needed</div><div class="w">Fisheye GoPro wrist view of one cup-saucer demonstration.</div><div class="s">wrist camera · 1920×1080 · ~10 s · mp4</div></div></div><div class="tile"><div class="label"><i style="background:#8b6fc4"></i>Hide</div><div class="na">Not applicable. The end effector is identical, so there is nothing to hide.</div></div><div class="tile"><div class="label"><i style="background:#3b8fb0"></i>Erase</div><div class="na">Not applicable.</div></div><div class="tile"><div class="label"><i style="background:#ee8a3a"></i>Replace</div><div class="na">Not applicable.</div></div>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="her-slide" role="tabpanel" aria-label="Recycling">
      <div class="head"><h3>Recycling: human hand to XHand</h3><p class="sub">Source: human hand with a wrist-mounted iPhone · Target: XHand with the same camera</p></div>
      <div class="her-pair">
        <figure class="clipw "><div class="clip"><span class="tag">Source demo · human hand</span><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"><source src="demo_hand_recycling.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div><figcaption>Source demonstration: a human hand wearing the wrist-mounted iPhone drops the carton into the bin.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="clipw ok"><div class="clip"><span class="tag">Target rollout · XHand</span><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"><source src="rollout_xhand_success.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div><figcaption>No adaptation finetuned on 25 XHand demos, same camera, 3× speed.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="her-quad">
        <div class="tile"><div class="label"><i style="background:#5b6472"></i>No adaptation</div><div class="ph"><div class="k">Media needed</div><div class="w">Wrist view of one human-hand recycling demonstration, unmodified.</div><div class="s">wrist camera · 960×540 or 640×480 · ~10 s · mp4</div></div></div><div class="tile"><div class="label"><i style="background:#8b6fc4"></i>Hide</div><div class="ph"><div class="k">Media needed</div><div class="w">Same demonstration with the hand patches dropped from attention.</div><div class="s">wrist camera · 960×540 or 640×480 · ~10 s · mp4</div></div></div><div class="tile"><div class="label"><i style="background:#3b8fb0"></i>Erase</div><div class="ph"><div class="k">Media needed</div><div class="w">Same demonstration with the hand inpainted away.</div><div class="s">wrist camera · 960×540 or 640×480 · ~10 s · mp4</div></div></div><div class="tile"><div class="label"><i style="background:#ee8a3a"></i>Replace</div><div class="ph"><div class="k">Media needed</div><div class="w">Same demonstration with the XHand composited in place of the hand.</div><div class="s">wrist camera · 960×540 or 640×480 · ~10 s · mp4</div></div></div>
      </div>
    </article>
      </div>
      <div class="her-nav"><button class="her-arrow" type="button" data-dir="-1" aria-label="Previous task">&#8249;</button><span class="her-dots"></span><button class="her-arrow" type="button" data-dir="1" aria-label="Next task">&#8250;</button></div>
      <p class="her-hint">Swipe or use the arrow keys to move between tasks.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid four her-legend">
      <div class="tile"><div class="label"><i style="background:#5b6472"></i>No adaptation</div><p class="how">The source fingers stay in the frame. Nothing is done to the data or at inference.</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><div class="label"><i style="background:#8b6fc4"></i>Hide</div><p class="how">The finger silhouette, rendered from the jaw width, is downsampled to the ViT patch grid and those tokens are dropped from attention (Cloak-style).</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><div class="label"><i style="background:#3b8fb0"></i>Erase</div><p class="how">Grounding DINO plus SAM 2 segment the fingers; an inpainting model (LaMa for zero-shot, MiniMax Remover for finetuning) fills the background.</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><div class="label"><i style="background:#ee8a3a"></i>Replace</div><p class="how">After erasing, a photograph of the target fingers at the matching jaw width is composited back in from a bank captured on the target gripper.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== Overview ===== -->
<section class="section is-medium" id="overview">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="title">Three things to take away</h2>
    <div class="takeaways">
      <div class="tk"><div class="tk-n">1</div><div class="tk-h">Leave the wrist view alone.</div><div class="tk-t">Untouched source data transfers to a new gripper better than any edited version, zero-shot and after finetuning. Hiding, erasing or replacing the fingers removes contact cues the policy needs.</div></div>
      <div class="tk"><div class="tk-n">2</div><div class="tk-h">A few target demos do the rest.</div><div class="tk-t">Finetuning on a couple dozen target demonstrations recovers most of the source data's value, far beyond what those demonstrations achieve on their own.</div></div>
      <div class="tk"><div class="tk-n">3</div><div class="tk-h">Camera or embodiment: either breaks zero-shot.</div><div class="tk-t">Swapping only the camera on an identical gripper, or only the hand under the same camera, is enough to rule out zero-shot transfer.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="container narrow" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
    <p class="subtitle">Policies trained on wrist-camera observations are coupled to the gripper that collected them and degrade when the gripper changes. Three families of remedies <b>hide</b> the end effector from the model's attention, <b>erase</b> it from the image, or <b>replace</b> it with the target gripper. We compare all three against the unmodified-data control under matched conditions, on four manipulation tasks and one case study, across five end effectors.</p>
    <p class="subtitle" style="margin-top:1.2rem">The surprising finding? <b>Leaving the source data untouched transfers best, zero-shot and after finetuning.</b> Methods that edit the wrist view narrow the visual gap but destroy the fine contact cues the policy needs, and that costs more than it returns.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== Summary video ===== -->
<section class="section" id="video">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="title">Technical Summary Video</h2>
    <div class="media"><video controls preload="metadata" playsinline poster="overview_poster.jpg"><source src="overview.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div>
    <p class="caption">Three-minute overview of the study: the four strategies, the five tasks, and the results.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== The four strategies ===== -->
<section class="section is-small" id="methods">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="title">Hide, Erase, or Replace?</h2>
    <div class="content narrow">
      <p>Every strategy sees the same demonstration and changes only the wrist image; the task viewer at the top of the page shows each pipeline side by side.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <div class="ph" style="aspect-ratio: 16 / 7">
        <div class="k">Media needed · replace bank sweep</div>
        <div class="w">Short clip of the target gripper's jaw sweeping open to closed on the wrist camera, then the indexed bank frames. Shows how Replace looks up the target fingers by jaw width.</div>
        <div class="s">1920×1080 · 8–12 s · mp4, no audio</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== Taxonomy (black band) ===== -->
<section class="section band" id="taxonomy">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="title">Method Taxonomy</h2>
    <div class="media"><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="taxonomy_poster.jpg"><source src="taxonomy_axes.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div>
    <p class="caption">Four axes, one at a time: where the intervention acts, what happens to the contact region, what it needs to know about the target, and how the visual gap is addressed. Replace is the only strategy that needs the target at training time; no adaptation is the only one that keeps the observed contact.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== Experimental design ===== -->
<section class="section" id="design">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="title">Experimental Design</h2>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">3,400+</div><div class="l">demonstrations collected</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">5</div><div class="l">end effectors</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">5</div><div class="l">tasks</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">~50</div><div class="l">policies trained</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">~1,500</div><div class="l">real-robot trials</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="media"><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"><source src="design_budgets.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div>
    <p class="caption"><b>Two budgets per task.</b> Zero-shot: the source policy runs on the target end effector with no target data. Finetune: the same policy is finetuned on 25 target demonstrations.</p>
    <div class="subsec">
      <h3 class="subtitle-h">Hardware</h3>
      <div class="content narrow">
      <p>Every UMI-family experiment uses the iPhUMI handheld interface: an iPhone in the UMI camera mount supplies the main and ultrawide wrist views, and the finger set swaps between three designs. The GenRobot case study uses that gripper's stereo camera pair, and the dexterous-hand study pairs a human hand wearing a wrist-mounted iPhone with an XHand carrying the same phone on an aligned mount. Policies run on a robot arm with the matching gripper attached; the wrist camera is the only observation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hw">
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Default UMI fingers</div>
        <div class="media"><img src="hw_default_umi.jpg" alt="Wrist-camera photo of the Default UMI fingers above a cup and saucer"></div>
        <span class="standin"><b>Stand-in</b> Wrist-camera photo. A three-quarter studio shot of the finger set, 2400×1800, would replace it.</span>
        <p class="how">The fingers from the original UMI. Source gripper for cup saucer, Push-T and drawer; target in the GenRobot and GoPro studies.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">UMI-FT fingers</div>
        <div class="media"><img src="hw_umift.jpg" alt="Wrist-camera photo of the UMI-FT compliant fingers above a cup and saucer"></div>
        <span class="standin"><b>Stand-in</b> Wrist-camera photo. Same studio framing as the others wanted, 2400×1800.</span>
        <p class="how">Wide, compliant fingers from UMI-FT. Source gripper for cup saucer and Push-T; target gripper for the drawer study.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">UMI-Air fingers</div>
        <div class="media"><img src="hw_umiair.jpg" alt="UMI-Air finger lying on a grey table, cropped from the lineup clip"></div>
        <span class="standin"><b>Stand-in</b> Crop from the lineup clip below, about 600×450. A studio shot at 2400×1800 would replace it.</span>
        <p class="how">Short fingers from UMI-on-Air, small enough to hook inside a drawer handle. Target gripper for cup saucer and Push-T; a source gripper for drawer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">GenRobot gripper</div>
        <div class="media"><img src="hw_genrobot.jpg" alt="GenRobot gripper with the stereo camera pair above the fingers"></div>
        <p class="how">Commercial gripper with left and right stereo wrist cameras and its own finger geometry. Source of the 765-demonstration cup-to-lid dataset.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">XHand and human hand</div>
        <div class="media"><img src="hw_xhand_hand.jpg" alt="XHand dexterous hand beside an open human hand"></div>
        <span class="standin"><b>Stand-in</b> Cut-outs at about 300 px. A single 2400×1800 photo of the XHand with its iPhone mount beside the human hand with the wrist mount would replace it.</span>
        <p class="how">Dexterous hand target for the recycling task. The camera mount is designed to align the wrist pose with the human-hand demonstrations.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Cameras</div>
        <div class="media"><img src="hw_iphumi.jpg" alt="iPhUMI handheld gripper with an iPhone in the camera mount and Default UMI fingers"></div>
        <span class="standin"><b>Stand-in</b> Shows the iPhone mount only. A photo with the GoPro in the same mount, 2400×1800, is still wanted.</span>
        <p class="how">iPhone main and ultrawide views feed the policy in every iPhUMI experiment. A GoPro fisheye in the same mount is the source camera for the camera-swap study.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="margin-top:2rem;max-width:640px;margin-inline:auto">
      <figure class="clipw"><div class="clip"><span class="tag">UMI finger lineup</span><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="hw_lineup_poster.jpg" style="aspect-ratio:16 / 9"><source src="lineup.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div><figcaption>The iPhUMI gripper body and its three finger sets: Default UMI, UMI-Air, UMI-FT. Fingers swap on the same body, so only the finger geometry changes between experiments.</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== Results ===== -->
<section class="section" id="results">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="title">Results</h2>
    <p class="rl-link"><a class="btn" href="rollouts.html">All rollout videos, every task &rarr;</a></p>

    <div class="task">
      <div class="text">
        <div class="q"><span class="qk">Question</span>Does editing the wrist view help a policy transfer to a new gripper?</div>
        <h3>Cup saucer: initial study</h3>
        <div class="eyebrow">Default UMI + UMI-FT → UMI-Air · prehensile and non-prehensile contact</div>
        <p class="answer"><b>No.</b> Untouched source data transfers best: 53% zero-shot against 27% for the best adapted version, and every method reaches the oracle after 25 target demos.</p>
        <div class="content">
          <p>Place a cup on a saucer with its handle facing left. When the handle points right, the policy must first rotate the cup with the outer surface of the open gripper, then grasp and place it. Source data: 592 demonstrations (290 Default UMI, 302 UMI-FT); target UMI-Air.</p>
          <p>Zero-shot, the unmodified source policy reaches 53% against a 93% oracle. The adapted strategies were expected to close the visual gap but widened it: hide 7%, replace 27%, erase 17%. With 25 target demonstrations every method reaches the oracle, using 5% of the oracle's data; 25 demos alone reach only 60%.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <div class="pair"><figure class="clipw ok"><div class="clip"><span class="tag">Success · +25 demos</span><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="rollout_cup_umiair_success_poster.jpg" style="aspect-ratio:16 / 9"><source src="rollout_cup_umiair_success.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div><figcaption>No adaptation finetuned on 25 UMI-Air demos: rotate, grasp, place with the handle facing left.</figcaption></figure><figure class="clipw bad"><div class="clip"><span class="tag">Failure · zero-shot</span><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" style="aspect-ratio:16 / 9"><source src="rollout_cup_fail.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div><figcaption>No adaptation, zero-shot: the rotation under-shoots and the handle still faces right.</figcaption></figure></div>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-row"><div class="media light chart"><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="chart_cup_poster.jpg" aria-label="Animated success-rate chart"><source src="chart_cup.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="task">
      <div class="text">
        <div class="q"><span class="qk">Question</span>Does the answer change when the task is non-prehensile?</div>
        <h3>Push-T: non-prehensile study</h3>
        <div class="eyebrow">Default UMI + UMI-FT → UMI-Air · non-prehensile contact</div>
        <p class="answer"><b>No, and the gap widens.</b> After finetuning, no adaptation reaches 77% while replace stays at 50%. Misleading contact cues are harder to unlearn than the visual gap they were meant to close.</p>
        <div class="content">
          <p>Push a T-shaped block into a printed T outline using only the outside of the open fingers, aligning orientation first, then sliding. Success requires no point of the block more than 1.5 cm outside the print. Source data: 1,572 demonstrations (770 Default UMI, 802 UMI-FT); target UMI-Air. Harder than third-person Push-T, since the wrist camera sees local contact but little of the block's global pose.</p>
          <p>No adaptation again gives the best zero-shot performance (43%), and here a gap stays open after finetuning: 77% for no adaptation against 50% for replace. The from-scratch control is 3%.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <div class="pair"><figure class="clipw ok"><div class="clip"><span class="tag">Success · +25 demos</span><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" style="aspect-ratio:16 / 9"><source src="pusht_success_crop.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div><figcaption>No adaptation finetuned on 25 UMI-Air demos, 4.5× speed.</figcaption></figure><figure class="clipw bad"><div class="clip"><span class="tag">Failure · erase</span><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" style="aspect-ratio:16 / 9"><source src="pusht_fail_crop.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div><figcaption>Erase finetuned on the same 25 demos, 8.3× speed: the block ends rotated off the print.</figcaption></figure></div>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-row"><div class="media light chart"><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="chart_pusht_poster.jpg" aria-label="Animated success-rate chart"><source src="chart_pusht.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="task">
      <div class="text">
        <div class="q"><span class="qk">Question</span>What if the source grippers solve the task in incompatible ways?</div>
        <h3>Drawer opening: conflicting source strategies</h3>
        <div class="eyebrow">Default UMI + UMI-Air → UMI-FT · conflicting source strategies</div>
        <p class="answer"><b>Keep all of them.</b> Filtering out the strategy the target cannot execute hurt. With the full source set the policy chose the feasible pinch anyway and reached 97% after finetuning.</p>
        <div class="content">
          <p>Open a drawer by its handle. Default UMI and UMI-FT are too thick to enter the handle, so they pinch it and pull; UMI-Air fits inside and hooks downward. We transfer to UMI-FT, which must follow the Default UMI strategy, and ablate whether the conflicting UMI-Air demonstrations are included in pretraining (78 Default-only vs 181 total).</p>
          <p>Filtering out the conflicting strategy hurts. With the full source, UMI-FT mostly took the compatible pinch strategy anyway and reached further toward the handle; after finetuning it always pinched, and no adaptation reached 97%.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <div class="pair"><figure class="clipw ok"><div class="clip"><span class="tag">Success · zero-shot</span><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" style="aspect-ratio:16 / 9"><source src="rollout_drawer_success.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div><figcaption>Zero-shot on UMI-FT from the full source set: pinch and pull.</figcaption></figure><figure class="clipw bad"><div class="clip"><span class="tag">Failure · commits to hook</span><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" style="aspect-ratio:16 / 9"><source src="rollout_drawer_hook_fail.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div><figcaption>Zero-shot: the policy picks the UMI-Air hook, which the wider UMI-FT finger cannot execute.</figcaption></figure></div>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-row"><div class="media light chart"><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="chart_drawer_poster.jpg" aria-label="Animated success-rate chart"><source src="chart_drawer.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="task">
      <div class="text">
        <div class="q"><span class="qk">Question</span>Does source data from a commercial robot dataset transfer to a UMI-style gripper?</div>
        <h3>GenRobot to UMI: a case study in industrial data</h3>
        <div class="eyebrow">GenRobot → iPhUMI · camera and gripper both change</div>
        <p class="answer"><b>Not zero-shot, and only modestly after finetuning.</b> Zero-shot is 0%; 25 iPhUMI demos reach 53% against 47% from scratch, about what the source policy manages on its own gripper.</p>
        <div class="content">
          <p>Pick up a cup of any type in any orientation and place it on a box lid. A no-adaptation policy trained on 765 GenRobot demonstrations (stereo cameras, different fingers) is deployed on the iPhUMI gripper with Default UMI fingers, zero-shot and after finetuning on 25 iPhUMI demonstrations.</p>
          <p>Zero-shot is 0%. Finetuning reaches 53% against 47% from scratch, a marginal gain; the same pretrained policy on its own GenRobot gripper also reaches only 53%, so finetuning may have recovered most of the source data's value.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <div class="pair"><div class="ph ok" style="aspect-ratio: 16 / 9"><div class="k">Success rollout</div><div class="w">iPhUMI finetuned on 25 demos picks a cup and sets it on the lid.</div><div class="s">third person · 1920×1080 · 10–15 s · mp4</div></div><div class="ph bad" style="aspect-ratio: 16 / 9"><div class="k">Failure rollout</div><div class="w">Zero-shot GenRobot policy on iPhUMI, or a dropped cup after finetuning.</div><div class="s">third person · 1920×1080 · 10–15 s · mp4</div></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-row"><div class="media light chart"><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="chart_genrobot_poster.jpg" aria-label="Animated success-rate chart"><source src="chart_genrobot.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="iso">
      <div class="iso-head">
        <div class="q"><span class="qk">Question</span>What prevents zero-shot transfer: the camera or the embodiment?</div>
        <p class="answer"><b>Either one is enough to break it.</b> Two experiments change exactly one factor each: the camera on an identical gripper, or the end effector under the same camera. Both drive zero-shot success to near zero, and 25 target demos recover most of the source data's value in both.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="iso-grid">
      <div class="iso-col">
        <div class="iso-tag">Camera only</div>
        <h3>GoPro to iPhone</h3>
        <div class="eyebrow">GoPro → iPhone · identical Default UMI fingers</div>
        <p class="answer"><b>7% zero-shot, 87% after 25 demos</b> (oracle 93%). Same gripper, same scene; only the lens changes.</p>
        <div class="figs">
        <div class="pair"><div class="ph ok" style="aspect-ratio: 16 / 9"><div class="k">Success rollout</div><div class="w">GoPro-pretrained policy finetuned on 25 iPhone demos completing cup saucer.</div><div class="s">third person · 1920×1080 · 10–15 s · mp4</div></div><div class="ph bad" style="aspect-ratio: 16 / 9"><div class="k">Failure rollout</div><div class="w">Zero-shot GoPro policy on the iPhone reaching beside the cup.</div><div class="s">third person · 1920×1080 · 10–15 s · mp4</div></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-row"><div class="media light chart"><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="chart_gopro_poster.jpg" aria-label="Animated success-rate chart"><source src="chart_gopro.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div></div>
        <div class="content">
          <p>To separate the camera from the gripper, we return to cup saucer and hold the end effector fixed (iPhUMI with Default UMI fingers), recording source demonstrations with a GoPro in the UMI camera mount and deploying with the iPhone. The fisheye GoPro and rectilinear iPhone differ in field of view, distortion, and where the fingers fall in frame. With an identical end effector there is nothing to hide, erase, or replace, so only no adaptation is evaluated.</p>
          <p>Zero-shot, the GoPro-trained policy succeeds in 7% of trials on the iPhone. After finetuning on 25 iPhone demonstrations it reaches 87%, against a 93% oracle.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="iso-col">
        <div class="iso-tag">Embodiment only</div>
        <h3>Recycling with a dexterous hand</h3>
        <div class="eyebrow">Human hand → XHand · same wrist camera</div>
        <p class="answer"><b>0% zero-shot for every strategy, 83% after 25 demos</b> (10% from scratch). Same camera, different hand.</p>
        <div class="figs">
        <div class="pair"><figure class="clipw ok"><div class="clip"><span class="tag">Success · +25 demos</span><span class="spd">3×</span><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" style="aspect-ratio:16 / 9"><source src="rollout_xhand_success.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div><figcaption>No adaptation finetuned on 25 XHand demos, same camera, 3× speed.</figcaption></figure><figure class="clipw bad"><div class="clip"><span class="tag">Failure · zero-shot</span><span class="spd">3×</span><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" style="aspect-ratio:16 / 9"><source src="rollout_xhand_fail.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div><figcaption>No adaptation, zero-shot, 3× speed: the hand closes on the carton badly and the grasp fails.</figcaption></figure></div>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-row"><div class="media light chart"><video autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" poster="chart_recycling_poster.jpg" aria-label="Animated success-rate chart"><source src="chart_recycling.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></div></div>
        <div class="content">
          <p>Pick a drink carton from a table and place it in a recycling bin. Source demonstrations are collected by a human hand wearing a wrist-mounted iPhone (142 demonstrations); the target is an XHand dexterous hand with the same camera and a mount designed to align the wrist pose. All four strategies are evaluated with the human-hand variant of the replacement pipeline.</p>
          <p>Zero-shot, every condition is 0%. After finetuning on 25 XHand demonstrations, no adaptation again performs best at 83%; training from scratch reaches 10%.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== Data and checkpoints ===== -->
<section class="section" id="downloads">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="title">Data &amp; Checkpoints</h2>
    <div class="content narrow"><p>Every demonstration set used in the study and the no-adaptation checkpoints it produced. Datasets are released as zarr replay buffers in the iPhUMI format (main and ultrawide RGB, end-effector pose, jaw width); checkpoints are Diffusion Policy <code>.ckpt</code> files with their training config. Download buttons are greyed out until the release links are in.</p></div>
    <div class="warn"><b>The checkpoints will not work on your setup.</b> They were trained on our tasks, scenes, cameras and grippers only, with no in-the-wild data and no attempt at generality. They are released so the results in this study can be reproduced and the training recipe inspected, not as policies to deploy.</div>
    <div class="tablebox">
      <table class="dl">
