    <p class="dlnote">Hide, erase and replace variants of each source set (the CLOAK mask rigs, LaMa and MiniMax inpainted buffers, and the replaced buffers with their finger banks) and their checkpoints will be released alongside. Code for the conversion pipelines and the deployment stack goes in the repository behind the Code button.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== Takeaways ===== -->
<section class="section" id="takeaway">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="box">
      <h2 class="title" style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2.2rem)">What we take away</h2>
      <div class="content narrow" style="text-align:center">
        <p style="font-size:1.2rem;line-height:1.5">Demonstrations collected on the wrong end effector are a pretraining resource, not a liability: leave them untouched, add a few target demonstrations, and the policy transfers. Today's visual adaptation methods cost more than they return.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section is-small" id="paper">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 class="title is-3 left">Paper, Authors, and BibTeX</h2>
    <div class="authors">
      <div class="author"><img class="pic" src="author_jack.jpg" alt="Jack Goler" width="600" height="600" loading="lazy"><div class="name"><a href="https://jgoler.github.io/Jack-Personal-Website/">Jack Goler</a></div><div class="role">Stanford University</div></div>
      <div class="author"><img class="pic" src="author_austin.jpg" alt="Austin Patel" width="600" height="600" loading="lazy"><div class="name"><a href="https://austinapatel.github.io/">Austin Patel</a></div><div class="role">Stanford University</div></div>
      <div class="author"><img class="pic" src="author_chuer.jpg" alt="Chuer Pan" width="600" height="600" loading="lazy"><div class="name"><a href="https://chuerpan.com/">Chuer Pan</a></div><div class="role">Stanford University</div></div>
      <div class="author"><img class="pic" src="author_shuran.jpg" alt="Shuran Song" width="600" height="600" loading="lazy"><div class="name"><a href="https://shurans.github.io/">Shuran Song</a></div><div class="role">Stanford University</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-links" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <a class="btn" href="paper.pdf"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M6 2h8l6 6v14H6zM13 3.5V9h5.5zM8 13h8v1.5H8zm0 3h8v1.5H8z"/></svg>Download the paper (PDF)</a>
    </div>
<pre>@article{goler2026hideerase,
  title   = {Hide, Erase, or Replace? An Empirical Study of Cross-Gripper Policy Transfer},
  author  = {Goler, Jack and Patel, Austin and Pan, Chuer and Song, Shuran},
  year    = {2026},
  note    = {[venue to be added]}
}</pre>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== Q & A ===== -->
<section class="section is-small" id="qa">
  <div class="container narrow qa">
    <h2 class="title is-3 left">Questions &amp; Answers</h2>
    <h3>If no adaptation wins, why study hide, erase, and replace at all?</h3>
    <p class="content">Each prior method was introduced on a task where it helped, but none had been compared against the plain control under matched conditions across tasks and embodiments. The point of the study is that comparison. A practitioner with a dataset on one end effector and a robot with another now has a baseline to beat before paying for a transformation pipeline.</p>
    <h3>Why does editing the wrist image hurt?</h3>
    <p class="content">The region the methods remove or re-render is exactly where the policy learns to look: the finger-object boundary. Finger deformation, shadows, and the relative position of finger and object tell the policy whether contact is happening and where. Hiding and erasing delete that signal; replacement draws fingers that were never actually touching the object. The visual gap shrinks, but the contact signal is gone.</p>
    <h3>How do you handle different finger lengths between grippers?</h3>
    <p class="content">We move each gripper's tool-center-point to its fingertip and express relative actions in the source frame during training, then command the target's own TCP at deployment. On the drawer task this choice matters: deploying zero-shot on UMI-FT with its own TCP succeeds in 73% of trials, against 57% commanded to the Default UMI TCP and 50% commanded to the UMI-Air TCP.</p>
    <h3>How much does 25 target demonstrations buy?</h3>
    <p class="content">A lot, as long as there is source data behind it. On cup saucer, 25 demos alone reach 60%; with 592 source demos on other grippers they reach the 93% oracle. On drawer and recycling the from-scratch control is 3% and 10%, while the finetuned no-adaptation policy reaches 97% and 83%.</p>
    <h3>Would adaptation help at a larger scale?</h3>
    <p class="content">Possibly, but our results argue against expecting it. If a transformation does not help in the low-diversity regime we study, where the visual gap dominates, there is little reason to believe it becomes useful once embodiment diversity in the data grows and visual alignment matters less. We leave action-space representation and larger-scale mixing to future work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

